---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s4">
  <div class="num">04</div>
  <h2>Get Claude to write the request</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    You can describe the thing badly first. That is what the chat is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open your usual Claude chat and paste the prompt writer below. Answer its
    questions about the website. Say you want one page for your photos and bio,
    saved in the project folder you have just made. It should work without
    installing more software.
  </p>
  <p>
    The result has six headings. Goal says what you want. Context supplies the
    facts and materials. Constraints are the limits. What done looks like gives
    you something to check. Output format says how the answer should be
    delivered. Example gives it a reference, if you have one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the result before using it. Remove anything you did not ask for and
    correct assumptions about your photos or personal details. The chat cannot
    see your project folder just because Claude Code can. Tell it what is there;
    do not give it private files to save a sentence of explanation.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Prompt writer: paste into Claude chat'}
    code={'I want you to write a prompt for an AI coding agent that works on files on my computer. I am not a programmer.\n\nAsk me questions one at a time, no more than five, about the goal, audience, existing materials, limits and how I will check the result. Do not ask for secrets or private information.\n\nThen write a prompt under 250 words with these headings: Goal, Context, Constraints, What done looks like, Output format, Example. Under Constraints include "Don\'t finish until" followed by specific checks. Do not invent missing details. Use plain language. End with "Show me a short plan first and wait for my ok before changing files."\n\nAsk your questions before writing the prompt.'}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Save this prompt writer in a note for other jobs. For this website, compare
    its answer with the ready-to-use request in <a href="#s5"
      >Build your website</a
    >. Use that supplied request for this first build. If you adapt it with the
    chat's answer, keep its filenames, limits and checks. A small wording change
    later does not need another interview.
  </p>
  <p>
    This approach owes a lot to <a
      href="https://somethingbig.ai/prompting-ai-agents"
      >Matt Shumer's writing on briefing agents</a
    >. Give it the result you want and the limits, rather than guessing the code
    it should write.
  </p>
</div>
